Change(genesis='66602cd0dec37d58d03eec9518ca805a7ac38ea912d2b0c366a0b67b4b9f50b6', deps=(), event=Decided(decision='typedtooling', at=datetime(2026, 11, 19, 0, 0, 0), actor='owner', title='The merge train is ported to Rust as app/tooling in four pull requests, each deleting the shell it replaces', body="The merge train (`scripts/train.sh`) is ported to Rust as the workspace member `app/tooling` (`suzatary-tooling`, the binary `train`) in four pull requests: the verdict table (T1), publication (T2), the gate run and the stop path (T3), and build, land, record and the entry (T4). Each pull request deletes the shell it replaces, so one program reads each stored file at every commit. The gate's orchestration follows in three pull requests only if T1 to T4 together meet the bar: the train guards shrink by the tests that became types or laws, their run time falls, and the reading set does not grow.\n\nThe design note's twelve recommended answers are this decision. (1) Python stays ruled out. (2) The tool is a workspace member that depends on no other member, built with `-p` into its own target directory under `.git`. (3) `scripts/train.sh` and `scripts/merge-gate.sh` keep their paths, as launchers. (4) Every verb is self-hosting: a train builds its tool from its own tree, and the leak guard's matcher stays the deployed binary. (5) The stop path's gap is fixed in shell ahead of T1. (6) The gate is ported after T4, and only if the bar is met; its step table's placement column (G0) comes in shell whenever. (7) The stored formats under `.git/train/gates` and `.git/gate` stay byte for byte, so no cutover forces a re-gate. (8) `.git/train/skipped` goes in T4. (9) The test seams (`SUZATARY_TRAIN_*`) are a cargo feature, so the binary the train runs reads none. (10) `git` and `gh` stay subprocesses. (11) `scripts/gate-units.mjs` moves into Rust with the gate's planner (G2). (12) `scripts/deploy.sh` is ported next, after T4. Nix is not used for orchestration.", rationale="The owner approved the port on 2026-11-19, on the design note of pull request 559. 15 of the train's 47 fixes were about shell (9 shell, 6 mixed), against 2 of the gate's 50, and all three signal, trap and wait fixes and the six-hour hang were the train's. The train is 51 % orchestration, the gate 9 %. A minimal crate of the train's shape built cold in 12.4 s and as a no-op in 0.05 s in the triage shell, and rebuilt in 0.76 s after a change, against 45.8 s as a verb of the CLI. Python starts in 0.12 s with no build, but nopython rules it out and its types are a checker's opinion. Nix cannot express a push, a wait or a signal, and the gate's steps as Nix checks took 1,914 s against 1,062 s for a change to the CLI alone.", supersedes=()))